# POSITIVE control for COLUMN 5 of scripts/sanitize_sweep.sh's verdicts.tsv —
# the column that answers "did the leak check actually RUN?".
#
# THIS FIXTURE MUST NOT BUILD, deliberately. Every other control in this
# directory proves a detector fires on something that RAN; this one proves the
# gate can tell "ran and came back clean" apart from "was never measured at
# all". Before column 5 existed those were the same fact, and an allowlisted
# fixture that stopped building was reported as one whose leak was FIXED — with
# a delete-this-row instruction attached to a live defect (`todo/t1360`).
#
# It is a CHECK-time rejection, not a parse error, so the fixture still formats
# and still reads as Gorget; and it fails identically with and without
# `--sanitize`, which is what makes the sweep grade it BUILD_FAIL_BOTH rather
# than BUILD_FAIL_SANITIZE_ONLY. The self-test asserts BOTH: column 2
# BUILD_FAIL_BOTH and column 5 UNMEASURED.
#
# ⚠ IF THIS FIXTURE EVER STARTS BUILDING, that is a real finding: the type
# checker stopped rejecting an int-to-String initialization. Fix the compiler,
# do not soften the control.
int main():
    String s = 5
    print(s)
    return 0
